# Nathejk Evercall — Project Rules & Context

> **Greenfield.** Nothing but documentation exists in this repo yet. This file
> describes the *intended* shape of the service, so treat it as the design to
> build towards, not a description of code that is already here. Items marked
> **TBC** are not yet decided or not yet verified against the provider's API —
> confirm them before relying on them, and update this file when you do.

## Overview

**Evercall** is Nathejk's telephony integration service. Evercall is a Danish
VoIP platform; this repo is the adapter that turns its activity into Nathejk
domain events.

It does exactly two things:

1. **Ingest** — receives call information from the Evercall platform over HTTP
   (webhook) when a call arrives/progresses, and publishes it to NATS JetStream
   as call events.
2. **Fetch recordings** — some time after a call has ended, pulls the audio
   recording from the Evercall API, stores it, and publishes an event pointing
   at the stored audio.

That is the whole scope. It is a headless, single-binary Go service with **no
frontend and no admin UI** — consumers of the call events (e.g. hq) own the
operator experience.

Deliberate non-goals: no SPA, no user authentication/session handling, no
read-model for humans to browse, no outbound calling, no call control (the
service observes; it does not steer calls).

---

## Architecture

```
┌──────────────┐  webhook (HTTPS)   ┌───────────────────┐  publish   ┌────────────────┐
│   Evercall    │ ─────────────────→ │  cmd/evercall (Go)│ ─────────→ │ NATS JetStream │
│  VoIP platform│                    │                    │            │   "NATHEJK"    │
│              │ ←───────────────── │  ingest (HTTP in)  │            └───────┬────────┘
└──────────────┘  REST: GET record  │  fetcher (consumer)│ ←──────────────────┘
       │                             └─────────┬──────────┘   subscribe (call.ended)
       │  audio bytes                          │ store
       └───────────────────────────────────────┴──────────→ recording store (TBC)
```

One process, two halves that only communicate through JetStream:

- **Ingest (write side, HTTP-driven).** A webhook handler validates and
  normalises the platform payload and publishes a call event. It does no I/O
  beyond publishing, so it can answer the platform fast — the platform will
  retry on non-2xx, and a slow webhook causes duplicate deliveries.
- **Fetcher (event-driven).** A JetStream consumer reacts to call-ended events,
  fetches the recording from the Evercall REST API, stores the audio, and
  publishes a recording event. It runs on a delay/retry schedule because the
  recording is not available the instant the call ends (**TBC:** how long
  Evercall takes, and whether it signals availability itself).

Keeping the fetcher behind JetStream rather than inline in the webhook is the
central design decision: recording retrieval is slow, fails transiently, and
must be retried, none of which a webhook request can absorb.

### Event subjects

Follow the org pattern `NATHEJK.{entityType}.{entityId}.{event}`. Calls are
**not** year-scoped (unlike hq's entities), so there is no year segment:

```
NATHEJK.call.{callId}.received     # inbound call arrived
NATHEJK.call.{callId}.answered
NATHEJK.call.{callId}.ended        # with duration, disposition
NATHEJK.call.{callId}.recorded     # recording stored; carries its location
```

`{callId}` is the Evercall platform's own call identifier, so the same call maps
to the same subject across every event and a redelivered webhook is idempotent
by construction. Do not mint our own id for it.

Event payloads and their Go types belong in `github.com/nathejk/shared-go`
(`.../messages`, `.../types`) — this repo publishes them, hq and any other
consumer unmarshal them, and a type duplicated in both repos will drift.

### Raw payloads

Keep the provider's raw webhook body on the published event (or in a dedicated
`…received.raw` field) alongside the normalised fields. The platform's payload
is the only evidence of what actually happened, the schema is not ours to
version, and events are replayed — a normalisation bug is recoverable from a
retained raw body and unrecoverable without one.

### State

There is deliberately **no MariaDB read model**: nothing in this repo serves
queries to a human. The event log is the state, and per-call bookkeeping the
fetcher needs (attempt counts, "already fetched") lives in JetStream KV. Add a
database only when something genuinely needs indexed queries — and say why in a
PRD first.

