## Intended project structure

```
evercall/
├── docker-compose.yml       # dev environment (api + external traefik/jetstream)
├── docker/
│   ├── Dockerfile           # multistage: api-dev → base → build, prod (prod last)
│   ├── bin/init             # prod entrypoint (exec's the binary as PID 1)
│   └── init/api-dev         # dev hot-reload entrypoint
├── go/
│   ├── cmd/evercall/        # the single binary: wiring, HTTP server, mux startup
│   │   ├── main.go          # config from env, dependency wiring
│   │   ├── routes.go        # webhook + healthcheck routes
│   │   ├── webhook.go       # one handler per provider callback
│   │   └── app/             # transport helpers (json, errors, middleware, server)
│   ├── internal/
│   │   ├── evercall/        # Evercall REST client (recording download, auth)
│   │   ├── ingest/          # payload → domain event normalisation
│   │   ├── fetcher/         # JetStream consumer: recording retrieval + retry
│   │   ├── recordings/      # recording store abstraction (filesystem/S3)
│   │   ├── jsonlog/         # structured logger
│   │   └── vcs/             # build-time version embedding (ldflags -X)
│   └── go.mod               # module: github.com/nathejk/evercall
└── .github/workflows/       # build-and-publish → DockerHub (nathejk/evercall)
```

No `pkg/`, no `vue/`. Generic non-domain code belongs in an external module.

---

## Technology stack

- **Language**: Go (version per `go.mod`; dev container image must match)
- **HTTP router**: `julienschmidt/httprouter` (as in other Nathejk repos)
- **Event streaming**: NATS JetStream via `github.com/jrgensen/stream`
  (`jetstream`, `xstream`, `subject`, `metatagger`)
- **CQRS seam**: `github.com/jrgensen/cqrs` — take `cqrs.Publisher` /
  `cqrs.Consumer` interfaces, never a concrete stream client
- **Shared domain types & messages**: `github.com/nathejk/shared-go`
- **Recording storage**: TBC — filesystem volume in dev; object storage in prod
- **Upstream**: Evercall REST API + webhooks (base URL, auth scheme, payload
  schema and recording endpoint all **TBC** — document them in
  `internal/evercall` once verified against the real API, not from memory)

---

## External integrations

| Service | Purpose | Config |
|---|---|---|
| Evercall (inbound) | Webhook delivering call info | `EVERCALL_WEBHOOK_SECRET` (TBC) |
| Evercall (outbound) | REST API for recording download | `EVERCALL_BASEURL`, `EVERCALL_TOKEN` |
| NATS JetStream | Event bus | `JETSTREAM_DSN` (`nats://jetstream:4222` in dev) |
| Recording store | Audio persistence | `RECORDINGS_DSN` (TBC) |

The HTTP server itself takes `PORT` (8080 in the image — it runs unprivileged
and cannot bind below 1024) and `MAX_BODY_BYTES`.

Credentials never go in `docker-compose.yml` — dev values live in the gitignored
`docker-compose.override.yml`.

---

## Coding conventions

### Webhook endpoints

- **Verify the sender before trusting the body.** The webhook is publicly
  reachable; anything that publishes to the NATHEJK stream on an unauthenticated
  POST is an event-injection vector. Use the platform's signature/secret
  mechanism (**TBC**) and reject on mismatch.
- **Answer fast, publish once.** Validate, publish, `2xx`. No recording fetch,
  no downstream HTTP call in the request path.
- **Be idempotent.** The platform retries; assume every delivery can arrive more
  than once. Keying events by the platform's call id (above) is what makes a
  duplicate a no-op rather than a second call.
- Return `2xx` only when the event is durably published. A `200` on a failed
  publish silently loses a call, because the platform will not retry.
- Handle unknown event types by logging and returning `2xx` — an unrecognised
  new platform event must not become a retry loop.

### Go

- Single binary in `cmd/evercall/`. Additional workers are JetStream consumers
  in the same process, not new `cmd/` entries.
- All configuration is read from environment variables in `main.go` via
  `flag.StringVar(..., os.Getenv("..."), ...)` and passed down in a `config`
  struct. Never read env vars deeper in the call tree.
- Handlers use `app.WriteJSON` / `app.ReadJSON` / `app.ServerErrorResponse` —
  not bare `http.Error` or `json.NewEncoder`.
- Dependencies flow inward: nothing under `internal/` imports `cmd/`.
- Keep `go test ./...`, `go vet ./...` and `go tool staticcheck ./...` green —
  the dev loop refuses to restart the binary otherwise.
- Log with the structured logger; include the call id on every call-scoped log
  line.
- **Never log recording audio or full raw payloads at info level** — calls carry
  personal data.

### Testing

The two failure-prone seams are the provider's payload shape and the retry
behaviour, so these get tests before anything else:

- Normalisation: table-driven tests from **captured real payload fixtures**
  (`testdata/`), not hand-written ones — the point is to pin the provider's
  actual shape, including fields we don't use.
- Fetcher: `cqrs/cqrstest` fakes for publisher/consumer plus a stubbed HTTP
  client, covering "not ready yet", transient failure, and permanent failure.

### Language

The domain here is telephony, not the Danish scouting domain: use English terms
(call, recording, caller, extension). Danish names are only for Nathejk domain
concepts referenced from shared-go.

---

## Development

Everything runs in containers — nothing is expected to be installed on the host.
See the `docker-dev-stack` skill.

```sh
docker compose up -d
docker compose logs -f api
docker compose run --rm api go test ./...
```

External Docker networks `traefik` and `jetstream` are owned by the org infra
repo and must exist before starting; never define them locally.

The webhook must be reachable by the Evercall platform, which cannot reach
`*.local.nathejk.dk`. For local end-to-end testing either replay captured
payloads against the local endpoint with `curl`, or point a tunnel at it — do
not expose the dev container publicly. (**TBC:** whether Evercall supports a
per-environment webhook URL for a staging deployment.)

---

## CI/CD

GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/build-and-publish.yml`) builds the multistage
image and pushes to DockerHub (`nathejk/evercall`): `latest` on `main`, plus
`{branch}.{run_number}`. Pull requests build but do not push.

Build args `GIT_COMMIT`, `GIT_BRANCH`, `BUILD_NUMBER`, `BUILD_VERSION` are
injected into the binary via `-ldflags -X` into `internal/vcs`, so a running
container reports its provenance (`-version`, and the `/healthcheck` body).

The workflow builds with **no `target`**, so the Dockerfile's last stage is what
ships — keep `prod` last. There is no separate test step: the `build` stage runs
`go vet` and `go test`, so a red test fails the image build.
